sunset overdrive the recently announced  open-world action game from developer  insomniac Studios is a strange bird  indeed you're constantly bouncing on the  roofs of cars firing off explosive teddy  bears and generally causing havoc and  mayhem wherever you go all this is  wrapped up in a colorful punk-rock  aesthetic that as you're about to hear  draws its inspiration from some unlikely  sources together the style and substance  of sunset overdrive makes it a pretty  far cry from insomniacs more recent  games including the resistance series  and fuse so why the sudden shift from  cover based shooting to high-flying  action why jump from the apocalypse to  the awesome pocalypse to find the answer  and to learn more about the game we  traveled to insomniac headquarters to  speak with developers themselves and  take a behind-the-scenes look at the  making of sunset overdrive yeah I think  the first time we pitched this game it  was terrible there's an epic disaster  yeah it was basically we did it we did a  presentation we were like here's what  the game's gonna be like but we don't  wanna get bogged down in a story detail  so we're not gonna tell you who you're  fighting what you're fighting how you're  fighting but here's all the stuff that  we're really into here's like all this  crazy pop-culture mishmash for Halloween  masks talking about like like cultural  mashups and we had yeah literally slides  and slides of vintage vintage Halloween  masks and like Popeye cartoons I've got  so upset about that pop-up where you  can't talk about Popeye we've learned  over the years not to be afraid of  failure we fail all the time whether  it's on a grand scale or a micro scale  and but the key is never to stop trying  different things because then you  stagnate and you don't learn from your  failures and certainly on this project  we've got a lot of things that didn't  work we try things that maybe some  people felt would have been awesome and  other people felt like just didn't match  the either the tone or the basic  mechanics we were going for Marcus  Center while we were working on  resistance 3 you know had been talking  and they had this idea about a game that  they wanted to make  and and so that's really what it was it  was just an idea you know how would you  survive the end times  once the team at insomniac had rallied  behind a shared vision for the end of  the world or at least the end of Sunset  City they had to actually start making  the game if you read GameSpot's preview  for sunset overdrive that went up last  week by the way then you already have a  sneak peak of what this game will entail  but in case you missed it the basics are  thus energy drinks are evil okay okay  there's more to it than that as sunset  overdrive is all about mixing fast-paced  reversal with insomniacs signature  gunplay the result is a game that wants  you in constant motion dispensing death  as you hop between grind rails and off  of restaurant awnings  however making all these pieces the  shooting the rail grinding the unusual  the bouncy car roofs all fit together  into one cohesive whole wasn't an easy  task when the game really kind of  crystallized I think for me was when  traversal became started using traversal  in the game like it just wasn't about  walking around world and kind of you  know killing a bunch o deed it was about  killing o deed but doing in a really  stylish fashion like you know grinding  jumping up round and really you know  looking cool while you're doing it this  is a game where you actually interact  with the environment all the time and so  a lot more even than games like infamous  where you interact with the elements by  what climbing up buildings but most the  time you're up in the air you're out  doing stuff this is a lot more like Tony  Hawk where the entire world has to be  built with how the gameplay mechanics  work so there just ends up being so much  tied together that we really couldn't  box them up and kind of develop them  independently when working on weapons  and sunset overdrive you know we have to  take kind of an approach of looking at a  couple different things that take into  account how fast the player can move as  well as making sure that they're getting  a good experience out of the weapon  itself and so you know what that means  is we have kind of an immune system that  allows the player to be a little bit  looser to make up for the fact of how  fast that they're moving but at the same  time a lot of our weapons have you know  AOE aspects to handle how fast you can  move so you can be a little sloppier or  a little bit of auto targeting or homing  to help you be a little bit sloppier so  you can move really fast but you still  have to do most of the work and then it  gets you that extra 10  percent of the way there or we have  things that are like status affects  setting groups of dudes on fire or  freezing them things like that so like  really help to you know manage the crowd  a little bit we've got more interviews  in this game that we've had at any game  we've ever released yeah I mean it is  one of those things when you look at the  game you know some people might might  think that you know it's like a kids  game you know because it's so colourful  and but we wanted to juxtapose that with  with kind of a more mature aesthetic in  terms of the tone in terms of the you  know we call it a satire it's not really  a comedy game and I think the difference  between you know a comedy game and a  satire is you know we have this mantra  or beyond where we we said okay don't  try to make jokes make comments when you  embrace irreverence when you embrace  this idea that you're not being bound to  real world rules or trying to tell a  story that takes itself completely  seriously then anything's possible  and that's what's been really fun for us  on the ratchet series for example and is  absolutely a blast for everybody on  sunset overdrive because sunset  overdrive involves this world that isn't  playing by our standard rules where we  can take the story in unexpected  directions and do some crazy stuff armed  with its irreverent tone outrageous  weapons and insane visual style sunset  overdrive is shaping up to be a  refreshing departure from the CIO's more  recent more serious projects of course  there's still plenty more to detect in  this upcoming open-world action game so  be sure to stay tuned again spot comm  for all the latest updates on the game
 

